course: biology
lesson: 323
title: Eagles: golden eagles, harpy eagles, Steller's sea eagles and eagle eyes
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Eagles are big birds of prey: hunters with hooked beaks, huge feet and amazing eyes. They kill with their feet and use the beak only to tear food.
explain: Picture a golden eagle high over a mountain. It spots a hare far below, folds its wings and drops like a stone at well over 200 kilometres an hour, then swings its talons forward at the last moment.
explain: There are many kinds: the harpy eagle catches monkeys and sloths in the rainforest, the Steller's sea eagle with its giant orange beak fishes on frozen seas in Japan, and the rare Philippine eagle lives in shrinking forests.
explain: Eagles also fill films and stories with myths: the screaming «eagle» call is usually a hawk, and eagles snatching children are tales and fake videos, because even a big eagle can carry only a few kilograms.
fact: Golden eagles hunt hares, rabbits and marmots on open hills and mountains, and in a steep dive one can reach well over 200 kilometres an hour.
fact: An eagle's foot has four talons, three forward and one back, and the back talon is often the biggest and works like a thumb to pin prey.
fact: Eagles kill with their feet; the beak is for tearing food.
fact: Harpy eagles raise only one chick every two or three years, so they come back very slowly when they are hunted or their forest is cut.
fact: The Steller's sea eagle, with a giant orange beak, is one of the heaviest eagles at up to about 9 kilograms; it nests in Russia's far east and many winter in Japan, catching fish.
fact: Eagle eyes are about as big as yours, and the back of each eye has far more light-sensing cells than ours and two extra-sharp spots instead of one.
fact: The wild scream given to bald eagles in films is usually the call of a red-tailed hawk; a real bald eagle gives weak, high chirps.
fact: Eagles often use the same nest year after year and add sticks each time; a bald eagle nest in Florida was reported to weigh about 2 tonnes, as much as a car.
fact: A famous video of an eagle snatching a small child was a computer fake, and even big eagles can usually carry only a few kilograms.
fact: Only a few hundred pairs of Philippine eagles are thought to live in the forests of the Philippines, made rare by forest cutting and hunting; it is the national bird and protected by law.
fact: Kazakh eagle hunters in Mongolia have trained golden eagles to hunt foxes and hares for many hundreds of years, and they mostly use females because female eagles are bigger and stronger.
exercise: What do golden eagles mostly hunt? | Hares, rabbits and marmots | Big deer, bears and grey wolves | Fish caught far out in the deep sea | Fruit and nuts high up in the trees
exercise: How fast can a golden eagle go in a steep dive? | Well over 200 kilometres an hour | About 20 kilometres an hour | About as fast as a person runs | About 2,000 kilometres an hour
exercise: How are the talons on an eagle's foot arranged? | Three forward and one back | Two forward and two back, like a parrot | All four toes pointing forward | Five forward and none at the back
exercise: What does an eagle use to kill its prey? | Its feet and talons | Its sharp hooked beak | Its strong, wide wings | Its sharp tail feathers
exercise: How often does a harpy eagle pair raise a chick? | Only one chick every two or three years | Ten chicks every spring in one big nest | Two chicks every month | One chick in a whole lifetime
exercise: Why do harpy eagles come back so slowly when their forest is cut? | They raise very few chicks | They cannot fly very far at all | They eat only one kind of leaf | They sleep for years at a time
exercise: Which eagle has a giant orange beak and winters on frozen seas in Japan? | The Steller's sea eagle | The golden eagle | The harpy eagle | The bald eagle
exercise: How big are an eagle's eyes compared with yours? | About as big as yours | About the size of a pea | Twice as big as a football | Much smaller than an ant's
exercise: Why can an eagle see so sharply? | Its eyes have far more light-sensing cells and two sharp spots | Its eyes work like a telescope that zooms in and out whenever it likes | It sees with its feathers too | It has four eyes instead of two
exercise: The wild eagle scream in many films is really the call of which bird? | A red-tailed hawk | A seagull | A parrot | An owl
exercise: What does a real bald eagle sound like? | Weak, high chirps and giggles | A deep lion-like roar | A loud wild scream | It makes no sound at all
exercise: Why can eagle nests get as heavy as a car? | Eagles reuse them for years and add sticks each time | Eagles add a load of wet mud from the rivers every day | Eagles fill them with stones | Eagles build them of metal
exercise: Can a big eagle fly off with a small child? | No, even big eagles can usually carry only a few kilograms | Yes, golden eagles do it often, which is why parks have warning signs | Yes, it happens in parks every week | Yes, if the child is asleep
exercise: Why is the Philippine eagle so rare? | Forest cutting and hunting | It eats only rare fruit | It lays eggs only once in a lifetime | It was always a cartoon bird
exercise: Why do Kazakh eagle hunters mostly use female golden eagles? | Females are bigger and stronger | Females have louder voices | Females cannot fly away | Females eat less meat
test: «saw a vid of an eagle grabbing a toddler in a park, crazy» What is the best reply? | That famous video was a computer fake; eagles cannot lift a child | It was real, the child was on holiday and the eagle let go over a lake | True, eagles do it all the time | True, but only bald eagles
test: Count them: 2 golden eagles land on a rock. How many talons on all their feet? | 16 | 8 | 10 | 20
test: Count them: an eagle grips a fish with both feet. How many talons point backwards? | 2 | 1 | 4 | 6
test: In a film a bald eagle opens its beak and lets out a wild scream. What is the truth? | The sound is usually a red-tailed hawk; bald eagles chirp | Bald eagles scream louder than any other bird, so the film is right | The sound is a real eagle in pain | Eagles cannot make any sound
test: In The Lord of the Rings giant eagles carry people over mountains. What can a real big eagle usually carry? | Only a few kilograms | A grown person easily | A small horse or a cow | A car, if it is light
test: «why'd a hunter bother diving, just fly straight at the hare» What is the best answer? | Diving builds speed and surprise | Flying straight is faster than diving | Eagles are scared of hares on the ground | Diving helps keep the eagle nice and warm
test: What would happen to a tree holding an eagle nest that grows heavier every year? | Its branches may break or it may fall in a storm | It would grow faster and taller because of all the extra weight | Nothing, trees can hold anything | It would turn into a nest itself
test: Hunters shoot a few harpy eagles each year in a forest. Why does this hurt the harpies more than it would hurt sparrows? | Harpies raise one chick every few years, so losses are slow to replace | Harpies cannot see hunters coming, because their eyes are weaker than a sparrow's | Harpies are smaller than sparrows | Sparrows also eat harpy eggs
test: «eagles got eyes the size of footballs, that's how they see so far» What is right? | Their eyes are about as big as ours but packed with far more light-sensing cells | Their eyes are tiny and weak, so they find rabbits by smell instead of sight | Yes, their eyes are as big as footballs | They see far because they fly near the Sun
test: Why would an eagle that catches big fish need a huge beak? | To tear apart big, tough, slippery meals | To scoop the fish out from deep under the water | To sing loudly to its mate | To dig a nest in the ice
test: How could you check if an amazing animal video is real? | Look for news from trusted sources and experts who checked it | Believe it if it looks clear and sharp and has lots and lots of likes | Count how many likes it has | Believe it if your friend shared it
exam: Identify the part of the body with which an eagle principally kills its prey. | The feet, equipped with four talons | The hooked beak | The leading edge of the wings | The feathers of the tail
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the vocalisation attributed to the bald eagle in cinematographic productions. | It is customarily the call of the red-tailed hawk | It is the authentic call of the bald eagle, recorded in the wild | It is the call of the golden eagle | It is a sound produced entirely by machinery
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the vulnerability of the harpy eagle? | Its slow rate of reproduction impedes the recovery of its numbers following losses | Its large clutches of eggs render it vulnerable to overcrowding and to shortages of prey | Its diet consists solely of a single plant | It is incapable of flight
exam: Ascertain the reason for which falconers among the Kazakh people principally employ female golden eagles. | Females are larger and more powerful than males | Females are incapable of escaping | Females possess a superior voice | Females require no food
